        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Trimline Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/trimline-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are Trimline treadmills still made?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not for the US market. The American Trimline treadmills were built by Hebb Industries in Texas from the mid-1990s, and the repair and review sites that followed the brand all describe it as discontinued. A separate, current Trimline range is sold in New Zealand by Elite Fitness, but it is not the same product line.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Who made Trimline treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Hebb Industries, Inc., of Tyler, Texas, whose name is on the owner’s manuals. One account says brothers Bill, Joe and Bob Hebb started the business in 1994. Other sources describe Trimline as a Roadmaster spin-off later linked to Schwinn and Nautilus. The accounts differ, and none affects what an owner can do today.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where can I get Trimline treadmill parts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Specialist suppliers such as FitnessRepairParts.com list parts by Trimline model, and at least one treadmill repair business says it still supports the brand. Wear parts like belts, drive belts and lubricant are usually available. Many electronic and moulded parts are not, so search by the exact model number and suffix from the frame label.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where can I find a Trimline treadmill manual?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Mostly on public manual archive sites, where Hebb-published manuals for models such as the 1600.1 and 2600.1 can be found, and in document libraries holding the 7600 manual. Parts suppliers also list some owner’s manuals as orderable items. Use a related model’s manual with care: the safety and belt guidance usually transfers, the parts list may not.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a used Trimline treadmill worth buying?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only cheaply and after testing it running. A working Trimline can still be a decent walking treadmill, but there is no warranty and many electronic parts are no longer available, so a controller or incline fault may end its life. As a rough estimate, pay no more than the low hundreds for a good working example.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Trimline 7600 the same as a Sears Free Spirit?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The parts catalogue for the Trimline 7600.1 notes that it was marketed by Sears as Free Spirit model 122.309200. If your Free Spirit carries that model number, Trimline 7600.1 parts and the shared 7200.1 and 7600.1 manual should apply. Check the exact number on your frame label before ordering anything.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-14",
      dateModified: "2026-10-14",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
