          <a href="#new-zealand-trimline" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The Other Trimline: A Current Range in New Zealand</a>
          <a href="#parts" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Can You Still Get Trimline Treadmill Parts?</a>
          <a href="#manuals" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Finding a Trimline Treadmill Manual</a>
          <a href="#buying-used" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Should You Buy a Used Trimline Treadmill?</a>
          <a href="#repair-or-replace" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Repair or Replace an Old Trimline?</a>
          <a href="#what-to-buy-instead" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. What to Buy Instead of a Trimline</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you have searched for a Trimline treadmill, you probably have one in front of you — a
          machine that came with a house, a parent's old treadmill, a cheap listing on a resale site
          — or you have found a part number and want to know whether anything still fits. The short
          version is that Trimline is a legacy brand in the US: the machines have not been made for
          years, and owning one now is a question of parts, manuals and judgment rather than of
          buying new. The one exception is a different, current Trimline range sold in New Zealand,
          which we explain below so it does not confuse your search.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is Trimline Still Making Treadmills? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>No — not for the US market. Trimline treadmills were built by Hebb Industries
            of Tyler, Texas, from the mid-1990s, and production stopped years ago.</strong> Repair
            and review sites that tracked the brand all describe it as discontinued, one parts
            supplier says it still supports the brand with parts, and we found no current Trimline
            treadmill for sale new in the US. The Trimline treadmills sold today in New Zealand by
            Elite Fitness are a separate, current range, not a continuation of the American
            machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            That makes a Trimline treadmill a used-equipment question. Some Trimline machines were
            solidly built for their era, and an old treadmill that still runs well can serve a
            walker for years. But the parts that end a treadmill's life — the motor control board,
            the console electronics, the incline motor, the rollers — are the ones most likely to be
            listed as no longer available. For the best-known model we checked, the 7600.1, 18 of the
            44 catalogued parts are marked no longer available.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the practical advice is simple. If you own a Trimline that works, keep it maintained
            and keep it running; belts, drive belts and lubricant are still easy to get. If it has
            failed electronically, get a repair quote before buying parts, and be ready to replace
            it. And if you are thinking of buying one used, pay very little for it, test it running,
            and read our
            <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to buying a used treadmill</a>
            first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="history" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Made Trimline Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            The clearest fact is the manufacturer's name. Owner's manuals for Trimline treadmills
            such as the 1600.1 and 2600.1 are published under Hebb Industries, Inc., and repair
            references place Hebb in Tyler, Texas. Beyond that, the accounts differ in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The founding account</h3>
          <p>
            One long-running treadmill review site says Trimline started operations in 1994, with
            brothers Bill, Joe and Bob Hebb opening the manufacturing facility, and that the aim was
            affordable, easy-to-use home treadmills. Another site says the first machine came off
            the line in 1994. Those agree with each other and with the Hebb name on the manuals.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Roadmaster and Nautilus account</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill repair business and a separate review site describe Trimline as a spin-off
            from Roadmaster, the American sporting goods company, bought out by its management and
            later sold to Schwinn, whose fitness business became part of Nautilus. A third site says
            the Trimline name is owned by Nautilus but that Trimline treadmills are no longer
            produced. We cannot verify the corporate chain from primary records, and the two
            accounts are not obviously compatible, so we give both and rely on neither for anything
            that matters to an owner.
          </p>
          <p>
            What does matter is this: Nautilus, Inc. later became BowFlex Inc., which filed for
            Chapter 11 in 2024, and Johnson Health Tech bought its main brands. We found nothing
            to suggest that any current company supports Trimline treadmills under warranty, and
            any original warranty on a machine this old will long since have expired. Our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand page</a>
            covers that ownership change.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where they were built and sold</h3>
          <p>
            The earlier numbered models were built in the US; one repair site refers to "the old US
            built Trimlines", and the Hebb manuals carry a Texas address. A review site describes the
            later T-series, such as the T335HR, as Taiwan-built. Trimline machines were also sold
            under other names: the parts catalogue notes that the Trimline 7600.1 was marketed by
            Sears as a Free Spirit model, 122.309200. If you own a Sears Free Spirit treadmill, check
            whether its model number maps to a Trimline, because the parts will be the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Which Trimline Model Do You Have?</h2>
<p>
            Everything that follows — parts, manuals, value — depends on the exact model number, so
            find it before you search for anything else. It is printed on a label on the frame,
            usually near the motor hood, the base of the upright or under the deck, and it often
            carries a suffix such as ".1" or ".2" that changes which parts fit.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The numbered Hebb models</h3>
          <p>
            These are the four-digit models from the Hebb era. Those we found in parts catalogues,
            manual archives and listings include the 1600.1, 2600.1, 7150 (with a 7150.2 variant),
            7200.1 and 7600.1. The 7600 is the one most often discussed; its owner's manual is
            shared with the 7200.1, and its parts list notes that two different motor shaft sizes
            were used during production, which matters if you are ordering a motor fan or pulley.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The T-series</h3>
          <p>
            The later models carry a T prefix. Review and repair indexes list the T305, T315,
            T325, T335HR, T340, T345, T350HR, T355, T360 and T370HR, and one site lists models up to
            the T380. "HR" generally indicated heart-rate features. Parts catalogues group several of
            these together for common parts such as drive belts — one listing covers the T305,
            T315, T325 and T335 — which helps, but always confirm against your exact model and
            suffix.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If there is no label</h3>
          <p>
            Labels peel off. If yours is gone, photograph the console, the motor hood and the frame,
            measure the belt (length and width), and note the motor's own label under the hood.
            A parts supplier or repair technician can often identify a model from that, and the
            belt and motor labels are what you need for the commonest replacements anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-zealand-trimline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Other Trimline: A Current Range in New Zealand</h2>
<p>
            Search results for "Trimline treadmill" now mix two unrelated things. Alongside the old
            American machines, Elite Fitness, a New Zealand fitness retailer, sells a current range
            of treadmills under the Trimline name.
          </p>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 Elite Fitness's own store listed Trimline-branded treadmills from
            the W1 walking model at NZ$499 to the A9 at NZ$4,995, including the W3, W5, TL1, TL3,
            A2.5, A7 and A8, as well as Trimline machines for weekly hire. It also publishes owner's
            manuals for its Trimline 1000 and Trimline 2000, which include parts lists and point
            owners to online forms for service, warranty and parts requests.
          </p>
          <p>
            We found nothing linking this range to Hebb Industries or to the American Trimline
            models, and it is sold in New Zealand, in New Zealand dollars, for New Zealand mains
            supply. For a US reader the practical point is to avoid mixing them up: a manual or part
            from the New Zealand range will not fit a Hebb-era Trimline, and the New Zealand range is
            not a way to buy a "new Trimline" in the US. If you are in New Zealand, treat it as you
            would any retailer's own-brand range and judge each machine on its published figures,
            warranty and local service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Can You Still Get Trimline Treadmill Parts?</h2>
<p>
            Some, not all. The pattern is the same as for most discontinued treadmills: common wear
            parts are still available, while model-specific electronics and moulded parts run out.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A worked example: the 7600.1</h3>
          <p>
            FitnessRepairParts.com, a large US parts supplier, lists 44 parts for the Trimline
            7600.1. On 14 October 2026, 23 carried a price, 18 were marked "No Longer Available" and
            three assemblies — the elevation, frame and upright assemblies — had no price shown.
            Still listed were the drive belts, a treadbelt, a console electronic board, a power cord
            and lubricant. Marked no longer available were the front and rear rollers, several
            controllers, the deck wear surface, the incline motor, the keypad overlay and the mat.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is a useful picture of what to expect. A Trimline that needs a walking belt, a
            drive belt or lubricant is straightforward. One that needs a motor controller, a roller
            or an incline motor may need a used part, a compatible generic part sourced by a
            technician, or a decision to retire the machine.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where to look</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Specialist parts suppliers.</strong> Search by exact model number and
            suffix. FitnessRepairParts.com has Trimline catalogues, and a treadmill repair
            business that stopped reviewing the brand says it still provides parts support.</li>
            <li><strong>Generic equivalents.</strong> Walking belts can be made to size, and drive
            belts, rollers and bearings are often standard dimensions. Measure the old part and
            quote its markings.</li>
            <li><strong>Local repair technicians.</strong> An independent treadmill technician may
            be able to fit a universal motor controller or rebuild a roller where the original part
            is gone.</li>
            <li><strong>Used parts.</strong> Parted-out machines on resale sites can supply consoles
            and moulded covers, but buy only when the seller confirms the model number.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            covers when a technician makes sense, and our guides to
            <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">replacing a walking belt</a>
            and the
            <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">drive belt</a>
            walk through the two jobs an old Trimline is most likely to need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="manuals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Finding a Trimline Treadmill Manual</h2>
<p>
            Trimline owner's manuals survive mostly on public manual archives rather than on any
            manufacturer's site. We found Hebb-published manuals for the 1600.1 and 2600.1 on manual
            archive sites, copies of the 7600 manual in document libraries, and owner's manuals
            listed as parts by FitnessRepairParts.com for the 7200.1 and 7600.1 and for the T325.1
            and T335HR.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to look for in the manual</h3>
          <p>
            Three sections of an old treadmill manual are worth reading even if you never assemble
            or repair anything. The lubrication section tells you whether the deck needs regular
            lubricant and which type — some decks were pre-waxed and need little, others need
            silicone at set intervals. The belt adjustment section gives the tension and tracking
            procedure for your model. And the exploded parts diagram gives you the part numbers
            that make a parts search possible.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If you cannot find yours</h3>
          <p>
            Use a manual for a closely related model with care: the safety, belt and lubrication
            guidance usually carries across, while the parts list may not. Where the manual and a
            general guide disagree, follow the manual for your model. Our guides to
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication</a>
            and
            <a href="/how-to-adjust-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt adjustment</a>
            give the general method. Always unplug the treadmill before removing the motor hood or
            working on the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Should You Buy a Used Trimline Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Only cheaply, only after testing it, and only if you accept that a major electronic
            fault may end its life. A Trimline that runs smoothly can still be a perfectly good
            walking treadmill; the risk is entirely in what happens when something fails.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it is worth</h3>
          <p>
            We do not have market data for Trimline resale prices, so treat this as a rough
            estimate. A treadmill of this age with no warranty and uncertain parts is worth little
            more than its usefulness to you in the next year or two. One used 7150.2 we saw listed
            asked $100. As a rule of thumb, pay in the low hundreds at most for a working example in
            good condition, and walk away from anything that does not run under power. Collection
            is part of the cost: older treadmills are heavy, and moving one safely takes two people.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to test one</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Run it for at least ten minutes, walking and, if you can, jogging, at several
            speeds. Listen for grinding, knocking or a rising whine from the motor.</li>
            <li>Take it through the full incline range and back. A slow or stalling incline is a
            common fault and the incline motor may be unobtainable.</li>
            <li>Check that the belt tracks centrally and does not slip when you push off.</li>
            <li>Lift the belt edge and feel the deck. A dry, rough or worn deck adds load to the
            motor; on the 7600.1, the deck wear surface is listed as no longer available.</li>
            <li>Find the model label and check parts availability for that exact model on your
            phone before you pay.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            goes through these checks in order, and our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used comparison</a>
            helps you decide whether the saving is worth the risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Repair or Replace an Old Trimline?</h2>
<p>
            The answer usually depends on which part has failed, because that decides whether the
            repair is cheap, expensive or impossible.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Fault</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usual fix</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Our general view</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slipping or worn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Adjust, lubricate or replace belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive belt worn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor brushes worn</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Replace brushes if the motor uses them</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Usually repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline not working</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline motor or its control</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair only if a part exists; otherwise use it flat</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No power, or cuts out</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor controller or console board</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Get a quote first; often replace</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rollers noisy or seized</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bearings or rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair if a technician can source them</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The rule we use for any old treadmill is to stop spending once a single repair
            approaches a large share of what a reliable modern replacement costs, and to treat
            electronic faults on a discontinued model as the likeliest end point. Our guides to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            and
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair or replace</a>
            set out typical costs, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            helps you narrow down the fault before you call anyone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safety on an old machine</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Trimline and
            Hebb on 14 October 2026 and found no recall. That does not make an old machine safe by
            default. Stop using any treadmill that smells of burning, trips a breaker, speeds up on
            its own or will not stop, and make sure the safety key and its clip still work. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">burning smell guide</a>
            covers the first of those.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What to Buy Instead of a Trimline</h2>
<p>
            If your Trimline has reached the end, or you were looking for one because you wanted a
            simple, affordable home treadmill, the same qualities are easy to find in current
            machines with warranties and available parts. Another discontinued line people still search for is <a href="/livestrong-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">LIVESTRONG</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            For simple, subscription-free walking and jogging, the
            <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101</a>
            is a long-standing budget recommendation, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$800 roundup</a>
            covers others at that level. For a sturdier machine that should last as long as a good
            Trimline did, the
            <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F63</a>
            pairs a 60-inch deck with lifetime frame and motor cover. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill brands guide</a>
            compares the companies that are making treadmills now.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Getting rid of the old one</h3>
          <p>
            An old treadmill that no longer works is heavy and awkward to dispose of. Scrap metal
            dealers, local council or municipal bulk collection and some retailers' haul-away
            services are the usual routes, and a working machine can go to a charity or a resale
            site. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">disposing of a treadmill</a>
            covers each option, and
            <a href="/how-to-disassemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to take one apart</a>
            explains how to get it out of the house.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Trimline treadmills still made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for the US market. The American Trimline treadmills were built by Hebb Industries in Texas from the mid-1990s, and the repair and review sites that followed the brand all describe it as discontinued. A separate, current Trimline range is sold in New Zealand by Elite Fitness, but it is not the same product line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who made Trimline treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hebb Industries, Inc., of Tyler, Texas, whose name is on the owner’s manuals. One account says brothers Bill, Joe and Bob Hebb started the business in 1994. Other sources describe Trimline as a Roadmaster spin-off later linked to Schwinn and Nautilus. The accounts differ, and none affects what an owner can do today.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I get Trimline treadmill parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Specialist suppliers such as FitnessRepairParts.com list parts by Trimline model, and at least one treadmill repair business says it still supports the brand. Wear parts like belts, drive belts and lubricant are usually available. Many electronic and moulded parts are not, so search by the exact model number and suffix from the frame label.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I find a Trimline treadmill manual?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly on public manual archive sites, where Hebb-published manuals for models such as the 1600.1 and 2600.1 can be found, and in document libraries holding the 7600 manual. Parts suppliers also list some owner’s manuals as orderable items. Use a related model’s manual with care: the safety and belt guidance usually transfers, the parts list may not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a used Trimline treadmill worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only cheaply and after testing it running. A working Trimline can still be a decent walking treadmill, but there is no warranty and many electronic parts are no longer available, so a controller or incline fault may end its life. As a rough estimate, pay no more than the low hundreds for a good working example.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Trimline 7600 the same as a Sears Free Spirit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The parts catalogue for the Trimline 7600.1 notes that it was marketed by Sears as Free Spirit model 122.309200. If your Free Spirit carries that model number, Trimline 7600.1 parts and the shared 7200.1 and 7600.1 manual should apply. Check the exact number on your frame label before ordering anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Trimline Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most problems with old Trimline machines come from ordering the wrong part or paying too much. These are the four to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering parts without the exact model number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Trimline models carry suffixes such as .1 and .2, and the 7600.1 alone used two motor shaft sizes. A part that fits one variant may not fit another. Find the label on the frame, photograph it, and search by the full number. If the label is gone, measure the old part and quote its own markings.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing the New Zealand range with the American machines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Elite Fitness in New Zealand sells current treadmills under the Trimline name. Their manuals, parts and model numbers have nothing to do with Hebb-era Trimlines. Make sure any manual or part you find refers to a Hebb Industries model before relying on it, and do not expect the New Zealand range to be available in the US.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a used one that is not running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A Trimline that does not power up, or will not change incline, may need a part that no longer exists. Never buy one on the promise that it only needs a fuse or a reset. Insist on seeing it run for ten minutes through its speed and incline range, and price it as scrap if it does not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spending more on repairs than the machine is worth</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is easy to replace a belt, then a drive belt, then a controller, and find you have spent the price of a new budget treadmill with a warranty. Decide your ceiling before you start, get a quote for electronic faults, and stop when a single repair approaches a large share of a modern replacement.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

